From 58240759a8fa691cb23d3b682eb3a7f46d12cbb3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 16:19:47 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10433/11362] Coerce the model alongside the agent
 (REQ-INFRA-6850)

`Codex CLI error: Model metadata for 'gemini-3.1-pro-preview' not found` has
fired 15 times across 5 dates since 2026-07-01, always exactly 3 per date -- one
task burning its whole retry budget and retiring for an environmental reason
rather than on its merits.

Cause: `task_model` is read at ~6579, the coercion block rewrites
`task_agent_type` in four places below it, and nothing touched the model. A task
planned `agent_type: gemini` + `model: gemini-3.1-pro-preview` was correctly
flipped to codex, then invoked with the Gemini model name.

The instructive part, and the reason this is a fix rather than a rollback: THE
SAFETY NET WORKED AND STILL CAUSED THIS. Uncoerced, the task fails as gemini and
is legible. Coerced, it fails as CODEX with an error that reads like a codex
problem -- which is why five occurrences went untraced. A half-applied coercion
is worse than none.

The invariant, now a pure function with its own tests: a coerced task never keeps
a model belonging to the agent it was coerced AWAY FROM. Two deliberate
directions:

  * Unknown models are KEPT, not replaced. Fail toward leaving the plan alone --
    a wrongly-kept model fails loudly at dispatch exactly as this incident did,
    while a wrongly-replaced one silently runs a different model and reports a
    result, so the artifact would claim a substrate that never ran.
  * Family membership is by NAME PREFIX, not a fixed table, so a future
    `gpt-6-*` or `claude-6-*` is not read as foreign. A table drifts narrower
    than its concept -- the failure mode this repository keeps rediscovering.

8 tests including one asserting the CALL SITE is wired, not merely the helper --
three times this session a feature's call site was replaced with a constant and
the suite stayed green.

Written twice: the first application of this patch was silently reverted by a
concurrent pre-commit stash (mechanism 1 of the concurrent-agents record) between
writing and verifying. `git status` showed the file clean and both patch scripts
had reported success. Reapplied and staged in one sequence to shrink that window.
Scope declarations cannot prevent it -- they bind what a session may STAGE, while
the stash operates on the working tree of whoever is committing.

